// (884,778 entries) and [NEWVAR] says tid0 is also the biggest creator of
// variables (+46,505 between two already-stalled ticks) — so the drain refills
// the list it is draining. The remaining unknown is the exact call site, and
// [esp] at entry gives it with no assumption about anyone's frame.
uint32_t g_newvar_ret_va[8];
uint64_t g_newvar_ret_n[8];
uint32_t g_newvar_up_va[8];   // who calls sub_60b0d0, the dominant creator
uint64_t g_newvar_up_n[8];
// ⚠️ A PROBE WHOSE ONLY OUTPUT IS CONDITIONAL IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A DEAD
// ONE. [UNLINK-WILD] printed nothing for a whole run and it took a one-shot
// guard probe to learn that its guard was fine, its block ran, and the silence
// simply meant "no wild unlink, and fewer allocations than my 200,000 periodic
// threshold". That is a clean negative and it read as a broken probe. Counters
// at file scope with a report at exit, like everything else here.
// ⚠️ AND THE SAME RULE APPLIED TO MY OWN SILENT PROBES. [RAISE-SITE],
// [LIST-WIPED], [FREELIST-CUT] and [SELF-CLEARED] have printed nothing across
// every run of this session. Their block provably executes — a one-shot
// [NEWVAR-GUARD] established that — so each silence is a RESULT: no allocation
// entered with an empty sentinel, the head never stepped to zero between two
// allocations, no push ever shortened the free list, and the watched slot never
// went to zero while holding a thread. None of that is legible from an absent
// line, which is exactly the trap this file keeps paying for. One report, five
// numbers, at exit.
// ── 🎯 [THREAD-INSTALL] — DOES THE THREAD ARRIVE ALREADY SELF-LESS? ─────────
//
// sub_87e2e0 installs the running thread: `movzx ebx, word[edx+0xc]` then
// `[0x2f44f08 + inst*20] = ebx` (0x87e36e-0x87e37c). op 0x0f later reads
// word[that record + 2] as the thread's self, and on the freeze path it is zero.
// [SELF-CLEARED] has already established that nothing zeroes it afterwards — 0
// events of "the slot goes to zero while it holds a thread", against 12 changes
// to that word overall — so the thread should already be self-less at install.
//
// This reads it there. Three guest reads per chainwalk return, and it counts
// rather than announces: "how many installs, how many with a zero self, how many
// distinct records" is the shape of the answer, and one loud line would only say
// that it happens at all, which is not in doubt.
